Huh? How, how, how do I, uh, get all the, um, I don't know if they have a name in the manual for these things, but like, you know, the, the pink orbs, like, how do I, how do I get all that stuff?

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:49.580
Um, you know, Adventures of Lolo 2, on the other hand, is.

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:53.420
Is the, you know, tell me if this sentence makes any sense.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:03:00.080
It is the seventh installment of the Japanese Eggerland video game series.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:09.900
It was the fourth game released in European countries and the second game released in the United States and Canada.

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.740
But Adventures of Lolo 2 was never released in Japan.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.620
What? That doesn't make any sense.

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:24.100
So wait, okay. But, Adventures of Lolo 2, in some way, like, had to have come out in Japan, right?

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:25.460
Like, as a different name?

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.000
Like, maybe it was a different number?

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:31.880
Yeah, so, great, great, uh, analysis so far, Sean, because you're right.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:38.260
In some way, it had to, it had to come out for it to be part of this Eggerland series.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.080
But actually, it's more confusing than you would think.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:47.580
It's not like, oh, Final Fantasy, like, Adventures of Lolo 2 is actually Adventures of Lolo 6 in Japan.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:48.880
It's not like that.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:59.180
Instead, these Adventures of Lolo games take, like, one half of one Eggerland game and another half of another Eggerland game.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.760
And they just, like, grab the puzzles from various games.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:07.520
And then they also, like, change the sprites a little bit, uh, to, to be, like, one cohesive look.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:11.980
So basically, they're just stealing the puzzles, but technically remaking the games.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:21.000
And so that's why Adventures of Lolo 2 never released in Japan, because they would, they wouldn't be new puzzles, but it would technically be a new game.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:26.700
So I'm guessing it's kind of like a, like a best of Eggerland is, like, Adventures of Lolo.

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.680
Right. Which, which are the good puzzles?

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:29.840
We'll, we'll just use those.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:34.540
I don't know if that's true. I don't know if they actually test the puzzles beforehand and be like, yeah, these are the ones people will like.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:36.780
I'm sure they just throw them in there, you know?

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:38.920
I'm sure they don't even test the puzzles normally.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:39.860
Yeah, they don't even design them.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:43.020
They just, they just put stuff down and hope that it's a puzzle.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:54.600
It definitely feels more confusing than most Japanese adaptation video games that we get over here in America, where it's just like, oh yeah, it's the same game, but they removed all the licensed stuff.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:59.300
Or, oh yeah, it's the same game, but two is three and three is four.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:14.240
Um, instead, this is really hard to keep track of because you actually have to play all of the, if you wanted to, if you're like a huge Eggerland fan, you'd have to play all the Japanese games first and then notice the repeated puzzles in Adventures of Lolo.

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:17.000
Uh, maybe it's like a publicity thing.

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:23.120
They're like, even our releases will be, like, it'll be puzzling to just understand, like, what these are.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.400
Now, now they'd have to make it like an ARG or something.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:28.680
Yeah. To do like that, you know?

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:35.800
Well, maybe just no one's went to, you know, the woods in Canada somewhere to dig up the floppy disk that had the original game on it.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:43.700
That reminds me of, like, when developers, uh, talk about their games nowadays and they're like, yeah, nobody ever found out about this secret.

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:48.620
Uh, and then, like, the whole community, like, comes together and tries to find out about that secret.

00:05:49.060 --> 00:06:00.780
