00:20:44.520 --> 00:20:46.180
It's not a new dimension, though.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:54.020
Like, I think it still keeps the, it still keeps the, the soul of the game, but adds a new dimension to it.

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:59.400
It's more so just like, uh, oh, this, in this version of Call of Duty, you have this gun.

00:21:04.860 --> 00:21:08.820
So what is your puzzle solving strategy for a game like this?

00:21:08.920 --> 00:21:12.660
Without, you know, you don't have to give me a specific level and stuff like that.

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:24.940
But for a level where you do get stuck, where it's not as obvious as, like, oh, well, I just have to push this block to avoid this enemy that would immediately kill me so that I can go around him and get a heart.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:31.000
It's like, how do you solve a situation where you actually don't know immediately what to do?

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:32.000
What is your method?

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:34.000
I don't really have a method.

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:42.840
Um, I just poke at stuff, you know, try not to poke at it too much or you might softlock yourself and then have to burn a life.

00:21:43.200 --> 00:21:45.280
Um, and those are precious.

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:59.640
Um, but you know, you, you end up learning what each enemy can do and, uh, you try and get a bird's eye view, uh, get, get some altitude on the situation, you know?

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:03.560
And then, um, and then you just keep poking.

00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:04.860
I mean, that's what I do.

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:14.700
Yeah, I feel like it, it encourages me to, like, play in a way I normally don't play, which is, like, actually to, to play slowly, you know?

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:18.840
Like, I don't know, at first I was like, I'll just try this and I'll try that.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:25.280
But, like, losing those lives over and over again, it doesn't send you back too far, but sometimes it sends you back far enough that it's annoying.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:33.680
Yeah. Because, like, sending you back is, at first to me, is, like, a weird punishment in this because it's like, well, I already solved this puzzle so I can just do it again.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:36.060
It's just time that I'm wasting now.

00:22:36.340 --> 00:22:49.080
Some of these, uh, rooms, these puzzle rooms, like, there's, there are pretty obvious, like, patterns that can emerge that are like, okay, the theme of this one is X or Y.

00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:55.460
Like, you can see, like, oh, there's a row of these Medusas in this section.

00:22:55.460 --> 00:23:02.020
So, obviously, trying to figure a way, figure out a way to make that safe is gonna play into this.

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:06.340
And, oh, there's a lot of just those cute caterpillars around.

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:13.340
So, probably gonna have to move them and, uh, and then, like, oh, those stupid stone men are here.

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:14.980
I might just turn the game off.

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:23.880
So, stuff like that, you know, like, you'll, you'll get an idea just by looking at the arrangement of how, or, like, where to start, at least.

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:28.740
Yeah, and I think that became my strategy, like, over time, once I kind of got used to it.

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:33.140
It was just, like, try and do as much as I can in my head before I start acting.

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:34.860
Which is hard.

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:39.240
Like, like, that, that's, at least for me, it's hard for me to visualize that stuff.

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:48.900
But I do feel like the game, because at first, not to, like, change this question into now, like, a question about the, the system of losing lives.

00:23:48.900 --> 00:23:55.860
